 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Chosen over five-second HTTPS polling and hosted realtime services.
+
+  **Why this tradeoff:** The goal is subsecond update delivery to 800 simultaneous browsers during market hours (opening bursts of 20 edits/sec). Polling at 5-second intervals meets the 15-second freshness target but wastes read capacity and adds perceptible lag. Hosted realtime (e.g., Pusher, Ably) eliminates connection ops but introduces recurring per-connection costs and provider lock-in for a core product loop.
+
+  Self-hosted WebSockets accept operational burden: we own connection capacity planning, TLS termination, horizontal scaling of socket servers, and client reconnect/catch-up logic. In exchange we get:
+  - True push delivery with ~50ms latency
+  - No per-message or per-connection vendor fees
+  - Full control over protocol, backpressure, and replay semantics
+  - The publication outbox in the change store becomes the single source of truth for both initial sync and incremental delivery
+
+  This choice is reversible only with a coordinated change across change-store (outbox schema), delivery (session management), and browser-sync (reconnect protocol) — it is a cross-cutting architectural commit.
